 across the world to connect to  each other we have to have really fast  performance and having our data centers  located in the US you know doesn't help  us with this even though we use CD ends  - you know for our static content we  have a lot of dynamic content that we  need to generate in the data centers or  on site and so having a global presence  with them provide a cloud provider  really is going to enable us to improve  our performance and so the decision was  made that we're gonna look for a cloud  provider partner but we really wanted  one that fit with our culture as I  mentioned Etsy has a really strong  engineering culture then we wanted one  that we could partner with and really  really believe that they get our culture  we wanted one the focus on machine  learning  you know I describe the marketplace  really as an iceberg and the marketplace  is the tip of the iceberg and behind it  what you don't see is all of those  billion events that get processed every  single day in our big data  infrastructure and then all of that  machine learning that we you know I  mentioned one small application of it  but we're applying it in lots of ways  and we're gonna keep applying it more  and more and so we wanted someone who  had a real focus on machine learning we  also one of those international data  centers so that we can have points of  presidents around the world and then of  course that strong commitment to  environmental sustainability we want  someone who really understood what we  meant and how we felt about it and could  partner with that so the question was  well how do you decide who fits all of  these criteria so we took one big  project of migrate to the cloud and  broke it out into a bunch of smaller  projects and we have you know even  though it's a single marketplace we have  hundreds of systems within the  marketplace and within the Big Data the  machine learning and all of these that  power that and so we broke that into all  of these sub projects we used a racy  model to assign ownership of those and  we asked those owners to take that  vision of that system through an  architecture review some of these  systems were going to be ported lifted  and shifted some of them are gonna use  new technologies some of them were gonna  be redesigned completely to be cloud  ready  and so we one of the owners of these  systems to take through the architecture  review process and what came out of that  is a set of requirements thousands of  requirements from all of these sub  projects of what they would need going  to the cloud and out of the datacenter  and so we took all of those and used a  tool called a decision matrix we put all  the requirements into the decision  matrix and then we worked with our cloud  providers to do experiments and to do  lots of technical meetings and  architecture reviews with the different  cloud providers to get their input on  how best to design and implement our  systems but this decision matrix look  something like this  across the top we have our functional  requirements these are the things that  are overarching ly important to our  business the relationship the cost the  ease of use the value-added services  security locations all of these are  things that are really really important  and they're kind of overarching  requirements and then down the left  you'll see all of these sub project  requirements that people came up with  and there's literally thousands this is  just a you know a screenshot of that and  then we weighted them and we said these  customer requirements how did they  impact the functional requirements and  what we got out of that was a cyst was a  thousands of customer requirements that  were weighted so we knew that which ones  were the most important to us we then  used these weighted scores to have the  engineers rank the different providers  based on that and so as they went  through their experimentation or their  discussions they could use the decision  matrix to rank them and they could say  this fitness provider fulfills those  this divider doesn't at the end when you  cross multiply everything out you have  tens of thousands of data points that  you can compare these providers with and  in our case GCP had over 50,000 points  they won by over 10 percent and by using  this matrix we tried to eliminate  people's bias and bias because they the  preferred one provider or another or  they only had experience with one  provider or another and we tried to  eliminate all of that and make this a  very quantifiable approach that we felt  good with that we knew that they were  going to support our requirements and  meet our business needs and so we're  moving moving very quickly that we ain't  we announced the partnership in December  that we're gonna move to migrate  everything out of our data centers to  GCP we're on a two-year timeline we  almost immediately migrated all of our  image serving off of the of Etsy into  GCP and lots of the lots of other sub  projects the machine learning training  infrastructure is now migrated to GCP  and so lots of these sub projects  are already in stuff but we're planning  on a full two-year migration to get all  of our systems out of there  Etsy web is the big one and so we're  trying to break that down into as many  small pieces as possible to move the  marketplace up to GCP and we of course  have to do this around the busy times a  year typically ecommerce coming company  where we have you know lots of lots of  traffic in the q4 around holidays and so  all of that has to be taking the  consideration and time into this but we  already have all of this running in the  cloud and we're doing load of  performance testing on that right now  and so that's how we're building up  confidence is replaying traffic against  the GCP infrastructure with the etsy web  marketplace running there and so that's  all that's going into the migration  stuff of this and so just to recap  before we take some questions you know  we're a global marketplace we really  really believe in our mission of keep  Congress human and that means everything  that we do has to follow that and that  means not only our engineering but the  way we host the way we build our  products all of this we have a very  strong engineering culture and we wanted  someone a partner that could could could  meet us there and really you know fit  within our culture we did this you know  made the decision and are doing the  migration by dividing it up into a  number of small projects utilizing that  decision matrix so that we have a  quantifiable decision then we can feel  good about and stand behind that we made  the right decision and migrated to GCP  really allows us to do this in a  sustainable fashion that they really get  where we're coming from in terms of  having 100% renewable energy and and  sustainably and really you know we found  I think someone that's proven to be a  great partner in the migration with us  [Music]
 

